// BT-3406 review follow-up: hoisting only the cascade argument(s) that need
// it (an open-scope same-class class-method call) while a *different*,
// side-effecting-but-plain argument in the same message stays inline can
// reverse their observable left-to-right evaluation order — the hoisted
// one's preamble now runs before the whole `send(...)`, while the plain one
// only evaluates inline at call time. `generate_cascade_args` now decides
// once, for the whole argument list, whether any argument needs hoisting;
// if so every argument is hoisted (a plain one via a fresh `let` binding
// too), never just some — see `stdlib/test/cascade_order_preserved_test.bt`.
//
// The receiver (`b`) and log actor live in their own files
// (cascade_order_receiver.bt, cascade_order_log.bt), since a .bt file may
// only declare one class.

Object subclass: CascadeOrderPreserved

  class run -> CascadeOrderLog =>
    log := CascadeOrderLog spawn
    b := CascadeOrderReceiver spawn
    b add: (log record: #plainFirst) value: (CascadeOrderPreserved noteViaSelfSend: log); noop
    log

  class noteViaSelfSend: log :: CascadeOrderLog -> Symbol =>
    log record: #selfSendSecond
